Overview

In this lab, I practiced transferring files securely between my Mac and my Ubuntu Linux virtual machine using SCP (Secure Copy Protocol) over SSH.

This lab helped me understand:

secure file transfers
remote Linux administration
client/server networking
Linux file management
SSH-based communication
-----------------------------------------------------
Step 1 — Open Multiple Terminal Sessions

I kept one terminal window connected to the Ubuntu virtual machine through SSH while opening a second local macOS terminal window.

This helped me understand the difference between:

a local machine
a remote Linux system

-----------------------------------------------------


Step 2 — Create a Local Folder on macOS

On my local Mac terminal, I created a folder called transferfold to store files that would later be transferred to the Ubuntu VM.

After creating the folder, I navigated into it using the cd command.

This introduced me to:

local filesystem navigation
organizing files before transfer

-----------------------------------------------------

Step 3 — Create a File on macOS

Inside the transferfold directory, I created a text file called notes.txt.

After creating the file, I verified that it existed using the ls command.

This reinforced:

file creation
directory verification
Linux/macOS terminal consistency
-----------------------------------------------------
Step 4 — Transfer the File to Ubuntu Using SCP

Using the local macOS terminal, I transferred the notes.txt file to the Ubuntu virtual machine using SCP.

The transfer used:

my Ubuntu username
the VM’s IP address
the Linux home directory destination path

After entering my Ubuntu password, the transfer completed successfully.

This step introduced me to:

SCP (Secure Copy Protocol)
secure file transfer over SSH
Linux file paths
remote system communication
-----------------------------------------------------

Step 5 — Verify the File Transfer on Ubuntu

Inside the SSH session connected to Ubuntu, I used the ls command to confirm that notes.txt had successfully transferred into the Linux machine.

The file appeared in the Ubuntu home directory, confirming that the SCP transfer worked correctly.

This helped reinforce:

remote file verification
Linux terminal workflow
SSH session management
-----------------------------------------------------
Step 6 — Read and Edit the File in Linux

After verifying the file transfer, I used the cat command to read the contents of the file.

I then added text into the file using terminal commands and verified the updated contents afterward.
-----------------------------------------------------
This introduced me to:

reading files in Linux
editing file contents from terminal
Linux text manipulation
remote file management
